  "headline": "An oil shock is doing the tightening, and gold has stopped acting as a haven",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as a stagflationary squeeze delivered through energy. Brent at $111 is up 83% this year, and the 10-year yield sits just under 5%, up 82 basis points over the same stretch. Over the past month the growth side has cracked: copper is down 4% and bitcoin 4.5%, while oil rose another 14%.",
    "Gold is the tell. It has fallen 7% over the month to the floor of its 52-week range even as the 10-year eased 12 basis points, and gold falling with yields is not a growth-scare bid; it is liquidation into a firm dollar and high real rates. The dollar index sits at 90% of its yearly range because the US, an energy exporter paying 5% on its debt, is where capital shelters. Bitcoin holding up better than gold, with one coin buying 3% more bullion than a month ago, confirms the driver is dollar scarcity rather than fear.",
    "The month's bond rally and the steeper curve disagree. Yields fell as oil hit growth expectations, yet the 30-year gave back less and the 30y–10y spread widened 4 basis points, so the long end is pricing sticky inflation and fiscal strain rather than relief. The steepening gets the weight: today's 3.5 basis point rise in the 10-year alongside a 1.5% copper bounce shows yields and growth still move together, a reflation reflex rather than a disinflationary one.",
    "The news names the channel. Drop Site reports that Tehran expects a US-Israeli strike before the midterms, and MintPress describes an IRGC strike strategy in the Strait of Hormuz, the supply route behind oil's year; Drop Site's daily also cites Brent at $114, above the $111.66 close here. Markets have priced the grind but not a strike: Brent's realized volatility is only 0.6 times its own yearly median, a calm regime for a barrel this expensive. A direct strike would be bullish for Brent, bearish for long Treasuries through the inflation channel, and a headwind for the rupee, with USD/INR already up 0.7% today as India's import bill bites; the Canadian dollar, up 1% against the greenback this week on oil, is the one currency on this page positioned to gain.",
    "Netanyahu's threatened West Bank operation, per The Electronic Intifada and Drop Site, adds to regional risk but has little channel beyond sentiment. Gold is the open tension: an escalation should bring a haven bid, yet with the metal at its lows against a 5% 10-year, the real-yield headwind is weighted more heavily, and the yen's 0.8% gain today against a firmer dollar suggests the market is already choosing currencies, not bullion, as its hedge."
